{
  "validity": "pass",
  "quality": "adequate",
  "issues": [
    {
      "kind": "annotation",
      "location": "obligations[0].answer_attributes",
      "problem": "The prompt only asks for the message text ('pull up the text of that message'), but answer_attributes includes both messages.message_text and messages.channel_id. The channel_id is a supplemental identifying/answer field that was never requested in the prompt, violating the rule that supplemental identifying conditions must be requested, not secretly enforced.",
      "repair": "Restrict answer_attributes to [[\"messages.message_text\"]] for both candidate role sets, dropping messages.channel_id unless the prompt is revised to ask for channel context as well."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The design correctly builds a genuine underspecified case: two distinct workspace members named 'Robert' (Robert Walsh in #engineering, Robert Chen in #core-infra) each tada-react to a different message, with no delegated tie-breaking authority in the prompt, matching the underspecified resolution mode. The route (MESSAGE→REACTION→USER→CONVERSATION_MEMBERSHIP→CONVERSATION→WORKSPACE) is respected without collapsing into an unjustified shortcut, and the bot's channel memberships (C03IJKL9012, C_INFRA per channel_members) support the described access plan via conversations.history, reactions.get, and users.info — all real, documented APIs, with no reliance on unavailable workspace-name lookups or hidden timestamp fields. The two negatives (same reactor wrong emoji; same emoji wrong reactor) are well-motivated and don't leak the answer. The planned new messages/reactions not yet present in the base seed are acceptable at this design stage per the given allowance for compiler-created rows. The one substantive flaw is that the answer_attributes obligation smuggles in messages.channel_id, an attribute never requested by the prompt, which is an annotation-level defect rather than a structural validity failure, so the design passes with a required repair before compilation is treated as final."
}
